fact WorkItem(id: "WI-931", description: some(value: "Four free-standing carriers provide specs whose operations nothing can dispatch to: 12 measured UnbackedProviderOperation reports, true by check_provider_operations' own criterion, currently STAGED OUT of that check.\n\nMEASURED under WI-928 (any stdlib load):\n  anthill.geometry.Vec3                        VectorSpace.vec_add / vec_sub / vec_scale / vec_zero   (4)\n  anthill.persistence.filesystem.FileStore     NonMonotonicStore.retract / update                     (2)\n  anthill.persistence.filesystem.IndexedFileStore  NonMonotonicStore.retract / update, BulkStore.pull, QueryableStore.retrieve (4)\n  anthill.persistence.sql.SqlStore             NonMonotonicStore.retract / update                     (2)\n\nWHY THEY WERE INVISIBLE. check_provider_operations scopes itself to CONCRETE carriers via sorts_with_constructors, which reads SortInfo.constructors. All four are declared with the free-standing 'entity' keyword (spec 6.3), and until WI-928 that spelling emitted no SortInfo at all -- so they read as abstract/value-less and were skipped. The check's own doc used to name Vec3 as an intended skip 'since no runtime value can dispatch through it', which is false: Vec3(x: 1.0, y: 2.0, z: 3.0) is a value. WI-928 corrected the classification and the doc.\n\nWHY THEY ARE TRUE. Vec3's four operations are backed by RULES (rule vec_add(?a, ?b, ?c) :- ...) and WI-818 settled that a rule is not backing -- it is specification the SLD world resolves against, not something the evaluator can run. The three stores are implemented by the Rust host (rustland/anthill-core/src/persistence/*), but no anthill.realization.Implementation fact records that, so the host-carrier skip at the top of check_provider_operations cannot see them either. Either way the provision certifies an operation that dies at run time with UnknownOperation -- exactly the unsoundness WI-363/WI-818 exist to refuse.\n\nCURRENT STATE (WI-928): check_provider_operations skips carriers in load::free_standing_entity_sorts (SortInfo.kind = entity) and cites this ticket. That set exists ONLY for staging -- 6.3 makes the two spellings one declaration, so keying anything semantic on it re-introduces the divergence WI-928 removed. An eponymous LONG-form 'sort P { entity P }' is checked, as it always was.\n\nWHAT THIS TICKET DECIDES, per carrier -- these are stdlib semantics questions, not kernel ones:\n (a) THE THREE STORES: declare the host backing. The shape is a 'provides X language rust ... end' block with artifact + carrier mapping (see rustland/anthill-stl/anthill/bool.anthill) or an Implementation fact naming the target; this is the WI-876 / WI-886 host-operation-mapping family, and the store ops (retract/update/pull/retrieve) are effectful, so check what proposal 054's effect rows require of the declaration.\n (b) VEC3: either give vec_add/vec_sub/vec_scale/vec_zero runnable operation bodies on Vec3 (the rules stay, as the SMT-facing specification), or drop 'fact VectorSpace[Vec3, Float]' from rustland/anthill-stl/anthill/geometry.anthill. Note the rules are deliberately relational for Z3 (per-component Float arithmetic), so adding bodies must not disturb that.\n\nACCEPTANCE: the staged skip in check_provider_operations and load::free_standing_entity_sorts are BOTH deleted, the check runs over free-standing carriers, and the stdlib loads clean; cargo-test green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. A carrier that legitimately cannot back an op must say so in its declaration, not by being invisible to the check.\n\nSOURCE: measured while delivering WI-928 (2026-08-02)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-08-02T11:42:31Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-931", author: "claude", content: "CLI WRITE-PATH SMOKE (WI-931): written by the freshly-built binary, i.e. through the operation_map registration that replaced the six hardcoded eval registrations. persist + flush dispatch unchanged.", at: "2026-08-02T10:41:39Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-931", author: "claude", content: "POST-REVIEW CLI SMOKE: written by the binary built after the /code-review fixes (per-carrier realization + op_backed spec-op narrowing + Vec3 withdrawal). persist/flush still dispatch through the operation_map registration.", at: "2026-08-02T11:35:17Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-931", author: "claude", content: "DELIVERED. The 12 reports are closed at their source, the staging (load::free_standing_entity_sorts and its skip) is DELETED, and the check now runs over §6.3's free-standing carriers with nothing keyed on which spelling was written. Full workspace: 4016 passed, 0 failed.\n\nMEASUREMENT LADDER, staged one family at a time against the 12: Vec3 -> 8, the persistence binding -> 1, withdrawing BulkStore -> 0.\n\n(a) THE THREE STORES. The backing was REAL all along and only its DECLARATION was missing: the six storage operations were registered by hardcoded qualified name in eval/builtins.rs, a registry no load-time reader can see (op_is_executable's builtin leg reads the RESOLVER's kb.builtins; these sat in the interpreter's). They moved to operation_map clauses in a new rustland/anthill-stl/anthill/persistence.anthill, plus store_* entries in HOST_FNS. The store satisfaction facts moved OUT of stdlib into that host closure -- geometry.anthill's own precedent and proposal 038's rule: a fact Spec[Carrier] may only stand where the spec's operations are backed, and in a stdlib-only load NOTHING backs retract. That move is what fixed the ~200 stdlib-only test failures the first cut produced.\n\nTHE FIX I SHIPPED FIRST WAS WRONG AND /code-review CAUGHT IT, confirmed by my own probe. Keying the operation_map on the SPEC (Store / NonMonotonicStore / QueryableStore) DISABLED the very check this ticket restores: is_host_mapped_op is a flat set with no carrier dimension and op_backed tried the spec op FIRST, so ONE mapping backed the member for EVERY carrier of that spec. MEASURED: an arbitrary 'entity ZzNotAStore(v: Int64)' with 'fact NonMonotonicStore[ZzNotAStore]' LOADED CLEAN, and so did a reinstated SqlStore. That is WI-876's defect A restated -- being genuinely polymorphic answers defect B (a wrong answer at run time) and says nothing about A (a load-time claim that does not imply the operation runs).\n\nUSER'S CALL settled the shape ('we can introduce common abstract operations over state'): the operations ARE common -- one host function over the store's state, resolving the store VALUE to its registered mirror -- so they stay declared and mapped ONCE, and what is per-backend is only WHICH BACKENDS THE HOST REALIZES (a one-line 'provides FileStore language rust' block emitting the Implementation fact). op_backed now refuses a host mapping on the SPEC op as backing for a CARRIER. My FIRST attempt at that narrowing was position-based (drop spec_op from the candidate list) and CHANGED NOTHING -- sort_ops_lookup's inherited-default leg returns the spec's own member for a carrier that overrides nothing, so the spec op reaches the carrier slot too. Keyed on the SYMBOL instead. Both probes now refuse; pinned by a_spec_level_host_mapping_backs_no_carrier and reinstating_the_sql_store_provision_is_refused.\n\nSqlStore: provisions DELETED. No host implements a SQL backend in any form (rustland/anthill-core/src/persistence/ has the two file stores and nothing else), so there is no closure they could honestly move to. Per the user, relocating the whole sketch out of stdlib is WI-934.\n\nBulkStore: provisions WITHDRAWN from both file backends. pull has no anthill-callable implementation in EITHER registry; the Rust BulkStore::pull trait method returns parsed FILES and is driven from Rust. WI-932 restores it.\n\n(b) VEC3 -- OPTION (b), NOT (a), AND THE REASON IS A MEASURED REGRESSION I ALMOST SHIPPED. Giving Vec3 the four functional bodies WORKS: they evaluate, the check is satisfied, and the FULL WORKSPACE STAYED GREEN AT 4010. It also silently re-targets the imported short name. Driven, on a rule body doing 'import anthill.geometry.{vec_add}' and calling the relational form: 1 solution before, 0 after, no diagnostic -- the bare name binds to the new Vec3.vec_add/2 instead of the namespace rule vec_add/3. The fully qualified name still resolved, so the rules were intact; only the import moved. NOTHING CAUGHT IT because vec3_ops_test::vec_ops_callable_from_user_rule imports exactly that name and asserts only that the rule LOADS. I found it only because /code-review asked for a test that the two spellings AGREE -- the agents did not find this one. So 'fact VectorSpace[Vec3, Float]' is withdrawn, algebra_spec_test now asserts the ABSENCE and names WI-935, and WI-935 owns restoring it once the naming is settled.\n\nBLAST RADIUS BEYOND THE TICKET, both found by /code-review and both real defects the ticket exposed rather than created:\n * cpp-gen emitted TWO 'struct Vec3' for an eponymous sort with fields AND operations (§6.3/WI-926 make it ONE symbol) -- a C++ redefinition error on a header this generator produced. Fixed at classify_namespace (the three bands are now DISJOINT by contract) and emit_entity_struct_by_symbol (one symbol, one struct), NOT at the namespace walk: that walk is one of three callers, and the other two are the public by-name API plus traits_classes_in_namespace, which the CLI's codegen cpp-project uses to scaffold 'one controller per traits class' -- it was offering Vec3, a data type, as a controller target (MEASURED). New suite wi931_eponymous_struct_test with a LOCAL fixture, since withdrawing the Vec3 provision leaves the tree with no sort of that shape. My own control there caught me turning emit_traits_struct's empty-traits-class error into a silent struct; narrowed.\n * A namespace-level bracket-less 'fact Spec' is SILENTLY DROPPED (measured by dumping every SortProvidesInfo: 'fact BulkStore' in filesystem.anthill and 'fact QueryableStore' in sql.anthill produced NO provision, while their bracketed neighbours did), while docs/rust-forward-mapping.md documents that spelling twice as a FEATURE. -> WI-933.\n\nTESTS: 11 in wi931_free_standing_provider_backing_test.rs + 5 in wi931_eponymous_struct_test.rs. CONTROL DISCIPLINE MEASURED, not asserted: re-introducing pre-WI-928 blindness (dropping every SortInfo.kind=entity carrier out of the concrete scope) fails control_an_unbacked_free_standing_carrier_is_still_refused and NOTHING ELSE; control_a_backed_free_standing_carrier_loads passes either way BY DESIGN and says so, so the refusal is about backing and not about the free-standing spelling.\n\nREUSE (/code-review): three hand-rolled load sequences replaced by existing helpers (load_stdlib_kb, try_load_kb_with_files), and the SortProvidesInfo walk lifted to common::sort_provisions -- it was about to be a FOURTH copy. Two comments corrected that were false as written: stdlib.rs's entry contradicted the file it annotated, and sql.anthill claimed a guarantee the first cut had disabled (now enforced and pinned). Spec 6.3 gained the provider-check bullet, the long-form/short-name caveat, and the satisfaction-fact placement rule, which had no canonical home and was restated in four places.\n\nSKIPPED, with reason: consolidating the suite's per-test stdlib loads (~2.8s). wi896's header explicitly accounts for its five loads -- per-claim naming is what the repo pays for, and these claims are distinct.", at: "2026-08-02T11:42:21Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-931", author: "claude", content: "CORRECTION FROM WI-932. This ticket's delivery feedback says of BulkStore: 'the Rust BulkStore::pull trait method returns parsed FILES and is driven from Rust. WI-932 restores it.' Both halves are wrong, measured at WI-932.\n\nNOTHING DROVE IT. The only non-test .pull() call in the tree was IndexedFileStore delegating to its inner FileStore; no dyn BulkStore / T: BulkStore bound existed anywhere. The bridge that ever loaded a pull into a KB, pull_with_source, was deleted as dead code by WI-187's own simplify pass (ecae0b16). The CLI cold start scans, parses and loads the tree itself.\n\nWI-932 DID NOT RESTORE IT. It deleted BulkStore entirely -- the anthill sort, the Rust trait and both file-backend impls -- because a spec whose sole operation no host implements is a shape with no realization, not a pending gap. So the forward reference above is cancelled; do not read it as outstanding work.\n\nThe withdrawal of the provisions, which is what this ticket actually did, was correct and stands.", at: "2026-08-15T09:38:23Z")
